t up  it's called kovacs  over the next six months i'm hopeful  that all world leaders will commit  to a fair sharing arrangement after  we've gone through the present period of  competition to get as many people as  vaccinated as possible  in the end it's one virus affecting the  whole world  we're all at risk we need to deal with  it as a single issue  i think you know if you say things like  everyone deserves a fair share no one  would really disagree with that  i'm interested in what it actually means  in practice so at what point  do you think the uk should look to  potentially at sharing out vaccines with  other countries and who should we  prioritize  the priority for vaccines at the moment  everywhere is  people who are most at risk of getting  severely ill  or dying from this disease those are  people who are susceptible because of  their age or because they've got other  conditions  they're people who are highly exposed  for example because they work in health  care  that's the same situation everywhere and  we need a global policy to do that which  is perfectly feasible  what matters is moment that politicians  believe  that their primary duties to make sure  that they get vaccines to  perhaps everybody in their countries we  think that citizens can actually perhaps  talk to their politicians and say hang  on a bit you know we're actually part of  the world  and we think the first priority is to  make sure that everybody everywhere gets  what they need  we set up a scheme as i said called  kovacs 100 countries are ready to  receive vaccines they've gone through  preparation processes  we've got money to be able to buy  vaccines for them  we're actually authorizing the vaccines  as quickly as we can it's a longer  process  for the wh so than for national  governments so just to um  just to jump in i'm quite keen to  clarify you're talking about the  priority to vaccinate the vulnerable  but then potentially to look at  elsewhere and does that mean then that  do you think that once the uk has for  example  vaccinated those most at risk people  over 50 then  that's when we should start giving  vaccines to other countries i think we  should  i mean of course each each prime  minister  each um group of mps has to form their  own decisions  but it's really a question of of what  makes sense economically  what makes sense for society and what  how it will want to be remembered  in 10 or 20 years time you know do we  want to be remembered as a world where  those who had the cash could afford to  vaccinate their whole populations  and countries that didn't have the cash  had to cope with a possibly  increasing quite dramatically increasing  death rate  among their health workers i don't think  so i don't think that's how  any individual really wants to be seen  when they look at themselves  over the years and it's going to be time  i think for a  national and perhaps even global debate  about what the priorities should be  because in the end we are human and  humans need to be able to work with each  other whatever their race whatever their  ethnicity whatever their wealth  talking about the global response to  coverage you know the fact that no one's  safe until everyone is safe the economic  damage you talk about  is it sensible to limit travel at the  minute particularly of course given  at the new variance and if that happens  do we need to take a blanket approach  rather than just having certain  countries on a red list  i think the main thing about dealing  with covet right now is what we do  inside our  communities and inside our countries  there are two things that really matter  the first is to do everything we  possibly can to avoid  getting the virus and spreading it and  we know what we have to do  to be two meters away from each other  wear masks  isolate when we're sick and of course  practice very good hygiene  and now we've got the extra advantage of  vaccines which will  save lives and also protect health  services  but we also need really strong community  level  locally led public health services what  in britain is called  test trace trees and isolate but that  needs to be  really well working because you can't  deal with this pandemic without being  able to stop spread  and then once those are in place we will  continue to see  the numbers going down last point for me  do you think the uk's test and trace  program is working in a minute  well i think it needs to do better i  think all countries need to get their  test trace treat isolate programs  working really well  with strong local support you see you  can't do  this kind of work of contract tracing  and transmission interruption  if you just do it centrally you've got  to get locally with central support  last point on this one is i just want to  say how brilliant people are doing  throughout the world at the moment on  this pandemic  look at how numbers are coming down in  different parts of europe in the us  right now that they're coming down  because of people's  actions because of the actions of health  personnel  and i think we just have to sometimes  stop for a bit  and say we are doing amazingly we've got  to go on we can't stop  we've got vaccines to help us but in the  end it's our self-discipline  that's going to get this pandemic well  contained it is quite incredible really  when you think about it and  the sort of restrictions that we've all  been living under for the last year or  so no one would have imagined it  um just a short time ago absolutely  extraordinary  um i'm keen to talk a bit about uh the  vaccines  as well and there's a report in the  financial times this morning  about a study of 2 000 people which  seems to suggest the oxford astrazeneca  vaccine  is less effective against a south  african variant  it has limited efficacy in protecting  against mild  and moderate disease although important  to note that nobody  seemed to get um severely ill or died  have you seen the study i  know you concerned by it well i saw the  i saw the  press cutting that you're referring to  and yes  you know this virus like every other  virus will  vary a bit like you and me we look  different  and i mean um we and other people look  different so we have variations  and some of those variations are going  to be more viable than others so yes  we're going to see variations emerge  that are particularly dangerous from  time to time we know how to do with this  kind of problem the first thing is  that we use the same precautions as  we're using more generally  uh with regard to distancing and face  masking and so on  secondly we can adapt vaccines that the  scientists are good at doing that  and we will do it just like we do for  flu we'll do the same for coronavirus  so yes variations are worrying and  we have to be very conscious of them but  we know they're going to come and  they're going to go on coming and we  know  that the vaccines will continue to have  to be modified  and that's as it as it will be the  really important thing is that leaders  all over the world need to give support  to outfits like the world health  organization which is the only kind of  organization  that can keep a track on variants as  they emerge in different places  and can help with advice on things like  travel each country will have to work  out its own travel rules  uh that has to be based on their  understanding of the danger  but in the end it's what we do inside  our countries that will determine  whether or not we can keep this virus  contained and under control um  the uk took a bit of a risk didn't it in  delaying  the dose between uh two vaccines against  the advice of the double h so  according to the joint committee of  vaccination immunization the data so far  shows actually quite high levels of  protection after the first dose  undoubtedly saving lives they think has  the uk's approach been vindicated  well certainly what's happening with  this virus is we're learning all the  time  the world health organizations advice on  intervals between vaccine doses  was based on what the manufacturer did  in doing the  what we call phase three trials of the  vaccine  and then wh so in its committees really  has to  work on the basis of what manufacturers  have  told them but is it wonderful that it  has turned out  as a result of the uk's bravery frankly  that this extended uh intervals seems to  be associated with even greater  protection  that's how we're doing kobit at the  moment we're all  learning together different countries  are approaching it in different ways and  and within wh talking with experts from  the uk  we're really really uh but i think  benefiting  from from the willingness of uk  scientists and uk leaders  to tell us very precisely what they're  doing differently and why they're doing  differently and then we add it all up  and we learn from it so yes  i think the uk's approach so far at  least has been vindicated and yes  i think this is a great lesson for the  rest of the world thank you  thank you british scientists yeah  incredible what the  science has been doing absolutely do you  think the whk could actually change its  advice then on the spacing of the jabs  well that depends not so much on wh so  secretariat the people who work in the  headquarters and in the  regional offices it depends on the  committee of experts  some of them british that advise the  that committee is meeting pretty often  at the moment because there's lots of  vaccines coming on stream  and the committee has to look at the  vaccines and it will indeed  look again at the dose issue as a result  of the british experience  that's great and there's more going to  come through about these vaccines  over the coming weeks and months and we  as i say will benefit hugely from  what different countries are doing we've  never had this speed of advancement sort  of  doing and learning at the same time in  my professional life in  anything like this way yeah it's been  quite awe-inspiring really the speed of  it all  um the who is carrying out an  investigation into how kovid  started in china visiting sites the wet  market for example  the wuhan institute of virology has the  wha  received full cooperation from the  chinese  the situation as i'm being told  is that the cooperation has been  exemplary the visit was carefully  planned  all the sites that uh who people wanted  to visit  are being visited there is a openness  for  all kinds of communications to take  place and the work is going ahead  i think pretty well but it's  early days this is detective work and in  terms of  you viewers who study how detectives  work will understand  that you're looking at lots of different  leads and you're trying to pull together  different bundles of evidence  we're anticipating that those this will  take quite a lot longer  i've been involved in studies of earlier  jumps of viruses from animals or birds  to humans  it's never clean and easy there's never  a little signpost saying follow this  path  you have to really work it out and and  so what i'm going to say is  great cooperation and good results so  far  but it may take quite a lot longer to  get the answer  just finally um talk about the detective  work um can you  rule out the theory that the virus may  have escaped from the lab in wuhan  well the thing about theories is that  you have to have them  as a way to set up where the reason why  things might  be occurring in a particular way there  will be theories about how this virus  originated including  uh what we call a laboratory uh incident  and that has to be assessed i can't rule  anything out and i know the team on the  spot  as well as those they're talking to in  in china they're not ruling anything out  either  all options are on the table and  everything will be looked at

